The ratio of the measure of the arc to a full circle turn is

$$
\dfrac{m\angle{AOB}}{360^\circ} = \dfrac{m\overset{\frown}{AB}}{360^\circ}= \dfrac{90^\circ}{360^\circ} = \dfrac{1}{4}
$$

Therefore, if the area of the circle is $\mathcal{A}$, then the area of the sector will be

$$
\begin{aligned}
A_{S} &= \frac{1}{4}A \\
&= \frac{1}{4} \cdot πr^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Substituting the value of the area of the sector, we get

$$
{\begin{array}{rl}\displaystyle 9 \pi  & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} \cdot  \pi  {r}^{2} \\ \displaystyle 9 \cancel{\pi} & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} \cdot  \cancel{\pi} {r}^{2} \\ \displaystyle 9 & \displaystyle  = {\displaystyle \frac{1}{4}} \cdot  {r}^{2} \\ \displaystyle r & \displaystyle  = \pm  6\end{array}}
$$

Therefore,

$$
r=6\,\textrm{km}
$$

, since a distance can't be negative.

